insert into public.questions (id, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('q1-001',1,'1.1','mcq',1,$q$A company installs warning signs stating that the premises are under video surveillance. Which control type BEST describes the signs themselves?$q$,$q$["Deterrent","Detective","Corrective","Compensating"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Signs discourage attackers from acting - deterrent. The CAMERAS would be detective (they record activity). Corrective controls fix damage after an incident; compensating controls substitute for an infeasible primary control.$q$),
('q1-002',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A legacy manufacturing server cannot be patched because the vendor is out of business. The security team places it on an isolated VLAN with strict firewall rules and enhanced monitoring. What control type is this?$q$,$q$["Preventive","Compensating","Directive","Deterrent"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Compensating controls exist specifically to substitute for a primary control that cannot be applied - here, patching. The segmentation and monitoring don't replace the need to patch; they reduce the same risk in the patch's absence, which is the textbook definition of compensating. (It also happens to help prevent exploitation, but that's incidental to why it's classified this way.) Directive controls guide behavior; deterrent discourages attackers.$q$),
('q1-003',1,'1.1','matching',2,$q$Match each control to its category.$q$,$q${"left":["Security guard","Acceptable use policy","Firewall rules","Security awareness training"],"right":["Physical","Managerial","Technical","Operational"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Guards are physical (real-world). Policies are managerial (governance documents). Firewall rules are technical (enforced by systems). Training is operational (performed by people as part of day-to-day operations).$q$),
('q1-004',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$After a breach, an organization restores systems from backup and rebuilds compromised hosts. Which control type do the backups represent in this scenario?$q$,$q$["Detective","Preventive","Corrective","Directive"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Controls that restore systems/reverse damage after an incident are corrective. Preventive stops incidents beforehand; detective identifies them; directive mandates behavior (policies, signage).$q$),
('q1-005',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$An attacker modifies patient records in a hospital database. Which element of the CIA triad is MOST directly violated?$q$,$q$["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability","Non-repudiation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Unauthorized MODIFICATION is an integrity violation. Confidentiality would be unauthorized reading/disclosure; availability would be making data inaccessible; non-repudiation is not part of the CIA triad.$q$),
('q1-006',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$A ransomware attack encrypts a file server so employees cannot work. Which CIA element is PRIMARILY impacted?$q$,$q$["Integrity","Confidentiality","Authentication","Availability"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$The data still exists unmodified in content but is inaccessible - an availability impact. (Modern ransomware often ALSO steals data - that adds a confidentiality impact - but the encryption itself denies access.)$q$),
('q1-007',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A signed contract is disputed and the sender claims they never sent it. Which security concept, if properly implemented, defeats this claim?$q$,$q$["Confidentiality","Non-repudiation","Availability","Least privilege"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Non-repudiation - typically via digital signatures - proves the origin of a message such that the sender cannot plausibly deny it. Confidentiality hides content; availability keeps services up; least privilege limits access rights.$q$),
('q1-008',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which AAA element answers the question: which resources is this verified user permitted to access?$q$,$q$["Authentication","Accounting","Authorization","Auditing"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Authorization grants or denies rights AFTER identity is proven. Authentication proves identity; accounting records usage; auditing reviews records.$q$),
('q1-009',1,'1.2','mcq',3,$q$In a Zero Trust architecture, which plane is responsible for making the access decision, and which for enforcing it?$q$,$q$["Data plane decides; control plane enforces","Control plane decides; data plane enforces","Policy engine enforces; PEP decides","Implicit trust zone decides; adaptive identity enforces"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The control plane (policy engine + policy administrator, using adaptive identity and threat scope reduction) DECIDES; the data plane's policy enforcement points (PEPs) ENFORCE and carry the traffic. The other options invert or scramble these roles.$q$),
('q1-010',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A security team deploys a fake credentials file named passwords_backup.xlsx on a file share. Alerts fire whenever it is opened. What is this?$q$,$q$["Honeypot","Honeynet","Honeyfile","Honeytoken"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$A decoy FILE that alerts on access is a honeyfile. A honeypot is a decoy system, a honeynet is a network of them, and a honeytoken is fake data (like a bogus API key) whose USE reveals compromise - closely related, but the file-based decoy is the honeyfile.$q$),
('q1-011',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which physical control specifically prevents an unauthorized person from following an employee through a secure entrance?$q$,$q$["Bollards","Access control vestibule","CCTV","Motion sensors"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An access control vestibule (mantrap) uses two interlocking doors so only one authenticated person passes at a time - directly countering tailgating. Bollards stop vehicles; CCTV detects/records; sensors detect movement but do not block entry.$q$),
('q1-012',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$Before a firewall upgrade, the engineer documents exactly how to restore the previous firmware and configuration if the upgrade fails. What is this called?$q$,$q$["Maintenance window","Impact analysis","Backout plan","Standard operating procedure"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$A backout plan is the documented reversal procedure required before implementing a change. The maintenance window is WHEN the change happens; impact analysis assesses consequences beforehand; an SOP is a general operating procedure.$q$),
('q1-013',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A change to a database server unexpectedly breaks three downstream applications. Which part of the change management process MOST likely failed?$q$,$q$["Approval","Dependency analysis","Backout plan","Maintenance window scheduling"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Unforeseen downstream breakage means dependencies were not identified during impact analysis. Approval may have occurred but with incomplete information; a backout plan addresses recovery, not prediction; scheduling affects timing, not impact.$q$),
('q1-014',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$Which statement BEST describes an application allow list?$q$,$q$["All applications run unless explicitly blocked","Only explicitly approved applications can run","Applications are scanned before each execution","Users may install applications after MFA"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An allow list is default-deny: only approved software executes - the strongest execution control. A deny list is default-allow (blocks known-bad only). Scanning and MFA are unrelated to list-based execution control.$q$),
('q1-015',1,'1.4','mcq',1,$q$Which algorithm is a symmetric block cipher used for bulk data encryption?$q$,$q$["RSA","ECC","AES","Diffie-Hellman"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$AES is the standard symmetric cipher - one shared key, fast, ideal for bulk data. RSA and ECC are asymmetric (key pairs, slow, used for signatures/key exchange); Diffie-Hellman is a key agreement protocol, not a bulk cipher.$q$),
('q1-016',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$Alice wants to send Bob a confidential message using asymmetric encryption. Which key does she use to encrypt?$q$,$q$["Her private key","Her public key","Bob's public key","Bob's private key"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Encrypting with Bob's PUBLIC key ensures only Bob's PRIVATE key can decrypt - confidentiality for Bob. Encrypting with her own private key would be signing (anyone could decrypt with her public key); she never has Bob's private key.$q$),
('q1-017',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$What does a digital signature provide that encryption alone does not? (Choose the BEST answer.)$q$,$q$["Confidentiality of the message body","Integrity, authenticity, and non-repudiation","Faster transmission of large files","Protection against denial of service"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A signature (hash encrypted with the sender's private key) proves the message is unaltered (integrity), from the claimed sender (authenticity), and undeniable (non-repudiation). It does NOT hide content - that requires encryption. Speed and DoS protection are unrelated.$q$),
('q1-018',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$A security engineer must ensure that two users with the same password do not produce the same stored hash. What should be implemented?$q$,$q$["Key stretching","Salting","Digital signatures","Tokenization"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A unique random salt per password makes identical passwords hash differently and defeats rainbow tables. Key stretching slows guessing but identical inputs still match without salt; signatures and tokenization address different problems.$q$),
('q1-019',1,'1.4','mcq',3,$q$Which choice lists password-storage hardening from WEAKEST to STRONGEST?$q$,$q$["Plaintext, hashed, hashed+salted, hashed+salted+stretched","Hashed, plaintext, hashed+salted, stretched only","Plaintext, hashed+salted, hashed, stretched only","Hashed+salted, plaintext, hashed, hashed+stretched"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Plaintext is worst; a bare hash resists casual reading but falls to rainbow tables; adding salt defeats precomputation; adding key stretching (bcrypt/PBKDF2/Argon2) makes each guess expensive - the strongest practical scheme.$q$),
('q1-020',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$Which hardware component provides a per-device root of trust, secure boot measurements, and protected storage for disk-encryption keys?$q$,$q$["HSM","TPM","Secure enclave","Smart card"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The TPM is the motherboard chip serving that per-device role (e.g., protecting BitLocker keys, attesting boot integrity). An HSM is an organizational appliance for high-volume key operations; a secure enclave isolates computation in the CPU; smart cards carry user credentials.$q$),
('q1-021',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$A payment processor replaces card numbers with substitute values; the real numbers live only in a hardened vault. What technique is this?$q$,$q$["Masking","Hashing","Tokenization","Steganography"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Substituting sensitive data with a vault-mapped token is tokenization - stolen tokens are useless without the vault. Masking hides characters for display; hashing is one-way (payments need retrieval); steganography hides data inside other media.$q$),
('q1-022',1,'1.4','mcq',1,$q$Which technology hides a message inside an image file?$q$,$q$["Tokenization","Steganography","Hashing","Key escrow"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Steganography conceals the EXISTENCE of data inside other data (images, audio). Tokenization substitutes values; hashing fingerprints data; key escrow stores keys with a third party.$q$),
('q1-023',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$A browser needs to verify in real time whether a single certificate has been revoked, without downloading a large list. Which mechanism does this?$q$,$q$["CRL","CSR","OCSP","Key escrow"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$OCSP queries the CA about ONE certificate's status in real time. A CRL is the full downloadable revocation list (bulkier, staler); a CSR requests a new certificate; key escrow is key storage.$q$),
('q1-024',1,'1.4','mcq',3,$q$A web server includes a time-stamped, CA-signed proof of its certificate's validity during the TLS handshake, so clients need not contact the CA. What is this?$q$,$q$["Certificate pinning","OCSP stapling","CRL distribution","Cross-signing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$OCSP stapling has the SERVER fetch and present the signed OCSP response - faster handshakes and no client-to-CA privacy leak. Pinning hard-codes an expected certificate in the client; CRLs are pull-based lists; cross-signing chains CAs.$q$),
('q1-025',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$An administrator needs one certificate to secure www.example.com, mail.example.com, and vpn.example.com. Which certificate type is designed for this?$q$,$q$["Self-signed certificate","Wildcard certificate","Root certificate","Code-signing certificate"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A wildcard (*.example.com) covers all first-level subdomains with one certificate. Self-signed certs lack third-party trust; root certificates anchor CAs; code-signing certs sign software, not TLS endpoints. (A SAN certificate is the other valid approach.)$q$),
('q1-026',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$Why would an organization use a private CA instead of a public CA for internal services?$q$,$q$["Private CAs provide stronger encryption algorithms","Internal certificates need trust only within the organization, at lower cost","Public CAs cannot issue TLS certificates","Private CAs never require certificate renewal"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$For internal-only services, the org controls its own trust stores, so a private CA gives free, flexible issuance. Encryption strength is independent of CA type; public CAs absolutely issue TLS certs; every certificate expires regardless of issuer.$q$),
('q1-027',1,'1.4','ordering',3,$q$Place the certificate issuance steps in the correct order.$q$,$q$["Generate a key pair","Submit a CSR to the CA","CA validates the requester's identity","CA signs and issues the certificate","Install the certificate on the server"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Keys are generated first (the private key never leaves the requester); the CSR carries the public key + identity to the CA; the CA validates identity, then signs; finally the certificate is deployed. Remember: CSR before validation, validation before signing.$q$),
('q1-028',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A data center adds redundant power, RAID storage, and a standby site. Which CIA element are these MOST directly supporting?$q$,$q$["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability","Non-repudiation"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Redundancy exists to keep services accessible through failures - availability. It does not restrict access (confidentiality) or prevent tampering (integrity), and non-repudiation concerns proof of origin.$q$),
('q1-029',1,'1.4','mcq',3,$q$Which key-related practice makes brute-forcing a stolen password database MOST computationally expensive per guess?$q$,$q$["Using SHA-256 instead of MD5","Key stretching with bcrypt or Argon2","Encrypting hashes with AES","Storing hashes in a separate database"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Key stretching algorithms are DESIGNED to be slow and tunable, multiplying attacker cost per guess. SHA-256 is stronger than MD5 but still extremely fast to compute; encrypting hashes helps only until that key is stolen; separate storage is defense-in-depth, not per-guess cost.$q$),
('q1-030',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which deception technology would BEST reveal an attacker who has stolen credentials and is exploring cloud storage: planting a fake AWS API key that triggers an alert when used, or deploying a decoy server?$q$,$q$["Honeypot, because it simulates a full system","Honeytoken, because its use anywhere proves compromise","Honeynet, because it covers more systems","Honeyfile, because attackers open files first"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A honeytoken is fake data (credentials, API keys) whose USE is inherently illegitimate - any use is a high-fidelity alert wherever it occurs. Honeypots/honeynets require the attacker to visit the decoy; a honeyfile alerts on access but the API key follows the attacker out of the environment.$q$);
